!PCSX - PCSX ReARMed for RISC OS                    @VERSION@
================================

PCSX ReARMed is a Sony PlayStation emulator, written for ARM machines.
This is its libretro core with a native RISC OS desktop frontend: a
window you can drag disc images onto, full screen, sound through
SharedSound, the keyboard and USB game pads.

This is a TEST BUILD: it writes a log to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.pcsxlog (the
icon bar menu's "Log..." opens it). Please include it when reporting a
problem.


Requirements
------------
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with NEON (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4, 400,
  500; most Cortex-A8/A9 boards). Written for and best on a Pi 4.
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16+ and ARMEABISupport (PackMan installs both).
- For sound: SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager (ssb.zip from
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html). Without them,
  games run silently.
- For game pads: !USBJoystick (USBJoystick 0.23 or later), run before
  PCSX or from your boot sequence.


Playing
-------
Double-click !PCSX, then drag a disc image onto the icon bar icon or the
window. PCSX reads:
  .cue with .bin    (the usual rip of a CD; keep them together)
  .chd              (compressed, from MAME's chdman)
  .pbp              (PSP "eboot" images)
  .iso, .img
  .m3u              (a list of discs for multi-disc games)
  .exe              (PlayStation programs, e.g. homebrew)

A file with a space in its name is shown by RISC OS with a hard space;
.cue files that name such files are understood.

Click in the window so it has the input focus (the keyboard is only read
then). Menu over the window: Pause, Reset, Full screen, Scale, save
states and slots, Close game, Quit.

Keys (change them in Choices):
  Cursor keys     D-pad
  Z  X  A  S      Cross  Circle  Square  Triangle
  Q  W  1  2      L1  R1  L2  R2      3  4   L3  R3
  Return          Start              Right Shift   Select

  F2 / F4         save / load the state in the current slot
  F6 / F7         previous / next slot (0-9)
  F8              pause
  F11, Alt-Return full screen on and off; Escape leaves full screen
  F12             (as always: the command line)

Full screen is a window covering the screen, so the desktop keeps
running underneath; the menu still works there.


BIOS
----
PCSX has a built-in replacement for the PlayStation BIOS (HLE), which
runs most games. A real BIOS image works better with some games and
shows the boot screen; if you have one from your own console, put it in
<Choices$Write>.PCSX.bios (e.g. scph1001/bin or scph5501/bin). It is
used automatically.


Memory cards and save states
----------------------------
Memory card 1 is <Choices$Write>.PCSX.saves.pcsx-card1/mcd, shared by
all games, as on a real console; card 2 is pcsx-card2/mcd (other choices
in Choices). Save states go in <Choices$Write>.PCSX.states.<game>.


Game pads
---------
With !USBJoystick running, the first two pads are players 1 and 2. A hat
switch or D-pad is the PlayStation D-pad. Analogue sticks reach the game
if you set pad1.type to analog or dualshock in Choices. Pads differ in
how they number their buttons: set joy.log to 1, press each button, and
read the numbers in the log; then set joy.buttons to match.


Choices
-------
The icon bar menu's "Choices..." opens <Choices$Write>.PCSX.Choices.
!PCSX.Resources.Choices lists every setting and what it does; copy the
lines you want to change into your Choices and choose "Reload choices".
Window scale, sound latency, keys, pad types, joystick buttons, and the
emulator's own options (dynamic recompiler on or off, BIOS, region,
frame skipping, high resolution rendering, ...) are all there.


Speed
-----
The dynamic recompiler and the NEON renderer are what make it fast. The
title bar shows the emulated frame rate (test builds): 60, or 50 for a
PAL game, means full speed. If a game is too slow, try
pcsx_rearmed_frameskip_type: auto. If a game misbehaves, try
pcsx_rearmed_drc: disabled (much slower) to see if the recompiler is to
blame.


